    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000c000002', '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000001003', 'Thread-Safe Counter', $d$Several threads increment one shared counter. The starter loses updates because count++ is a read-modify-write that is not atomic. Make the counter thread-safe with synchronized so the final value is exactly threads x increments.$d$, 'MEDIUM', 'JAVA', $i$One line: T N (number of threads, increments per thread).$i$, $p$The final counter value as a single integer.$p$, $k$1 <= T <= 16, 1 <= N <= 100000.$k$,
     $s$import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    private static int count = 0;

    private static void increment() {
        int current = count;
        count = current + 1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);
        int threads = in.nextInt();
        int perThread = in.nextInt();

        Thread[] workers = new Thread[threads];
        for (int t = 0; t < threads; t++) {
            workers[t] = new Thread(() -> {
                for (int i = 0; i < perThread; i++) increment();
            });
            workers[t].start();
        }
        for (Thread w : workers) w.join();

        System.out.println(count);
    }
}
